    "narrative": "The core of our lineup today revolves around **Austin Riley**, who earns our highest efficiency score at 63.46. While he is relegated to the #7 spot in the order, his matchup against Nick Martinez is the most favorable on the board; the SP_xERA of 4.65 allows **Austin Riley** to capitalize on his +16% skill adjustment, making him our anchor against a mid-tier arm. Conversely, **Isaac Paredes** (49.25) and **Josh Naylor** (46.07) are forced into marginal territory. Both face elite arms in Wheeler and deGrom, respectively. While **Isaac Paredes** earns a +6% bump for his #3 spot in the order, the sheer SP_xERA dominance of Wheeler suppresses his upside. We are keeping **Josh Naylor** active despite the daunting 3.12 xERA of deGrom, as his power profile remains our best bet for counting stats even against a premier strikeout artist.\n\nThe most difficult decision today involved **Cole Young**, who sat on the bubble with a score of 31.44. Despite his profile as an elite stolen base threat, the interaction between his platoon disadvantage and the matchup against deGrom pushed him well below our 40-point efficiency floor. We prioritize preserving game caps for players with higher daily ceilings, and the volatility of deGrom\u2019s secondary offerings against a young bat like **Cole Young** rendered him a liability rather than an asset. With **Corbin Carroll**, **Jose Caballero**, and **James Wood** all unavailable due to scheduled off-days or rest, we are operating with a diminished pool, forcing us to lean on the established power of our starters rather than the high-upside speed of our bench. \n\nRisks today are centered on the elevated SP_xERA matchups for our primary bats. Facing deGrom is never ideal for **Josh Naylor**, and though we trust the talent, the potential for a low-scoring, high-strikeout game environment is significant. We are monitoring the lineups for **Mickey Moniak**, **Trevor Story**, **Brice Turang**, **Seiya Suzuki**, **Giancarlo 